# Claim resolution (R1-R5)

An issue accumulates markers: a **claim** by an agent, or a **release**. These
rules decide what those markers mean. They exist because three consecutive
patches to the resolution logic each fixed one scenario and broke another --
the `claimed` label survived a withdrawal (#400), a loser's release erased the
winner (#402), an abandoned claim outranked a fresh reclaim (#405). Every one
was a combination of (agents x claim/release x fresh/stale) the previous patch
had not considered.

- **R1 -- per agent.** An agent holds the issue while their own newest marker
  is a claim. Only that agent's release ends their hold. A losing agent
  yielding must never clear the winner's claim.
- **R2 -- legacy releases.** A release naming nobody predates identity-aware
  releases and keeps its old meaning: it clears every marker at or before its
  own timestamp. Every release already on a GitHub issue is anonymous.
- **R3 -- staleness.** A holder is stale when `now - max(claim time, last
  commit on its branch)` exceeds the TTL. Branch activity can only revive a
  hold, never end one, so only a holder that already looks expired costs a
  branch lookup -- which is what makes resolution affordable on `next`'s path.
- **R4 -- election.** Among holders the earliest **live** one wins: earliest
  because a race is settled by who claimed first, live-first because a fresh
  reclaim must never hide behind an abandoned claim. With no live holder the
  earliest stale one is reported, so `claims` can show it and `--force` can
  take it over.
- **R5 -- your own claim is a different question.** "Which claim is mine?" is
  not "who holds this?" and has its own door, `claim_for`. Answering it by
  election deletes another agent's branch (`cmd_release` cleans up the branch
  it is told about). It fails closed: no match, no cleanup.

## Enforcement

`tools/test_backlog_claims_table.py` is the executable form of these rules --
a table over the state space, not prose. **Change the table first.** An edit to
`tools/backlog_claims.py` that the table still passes has not been reasoned
about, and that is the failure this rule exists to prevent.

Every path that decides whether a batch is held goes through `resolve_claim`
-- `claims`, `next`, `cmd_claim`'s race checks, and the Next-move seed in
`tools/next_moves.py`. They must not disagree: the seed's `[backlog]` lane is
what an operator pastes to start work, so a batch it calls free while `next`
calls it held is a collision waiting to happen. A bare `active_claim`
staleness decision is a bug; `tools/test_backlog_branches.py` asserts against
one in `cmd_claim`'s source.

